SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-001","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_formal_presidential_authority","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","3B.*","4B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://en.kremlin.ru/structure/president","publisher":"President of Russia","publisher_date":"undated; accessed 2026-07-24","claim":"The official presidency describes Vladimir Putin as president and assigns the president responsibility for domestic and foreign policy and supreme command of the armed forces, establishing his identity and formal authority during 2022.","locator":"Headings 'President of Russia' and 'Status and Authority of the President'","period_fit":"Direct institutional record applicable throughout 2022.","attribution_limits":"Describes formal legal authority and officeholder identity; it does not prove that Putin personally directed every state action or enjoyed unconstrained practical control.","contrary_evidence":"The same official description places presidential acts within the Constitution, federal law, and a formally separated system of powers."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-002","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_invasion_decision_personalized","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/russia-eurasia/politika/2022/06/has-war-made-putin-a-burden-for-russias-elites","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace / Tatiana Stanovaya","publisher_date":"2022-06-16","claim":"Stanovaya assessed that Putin made the decision for war almost entirely on his own and without prior elite discussion or preparation, making the invasion unusually attributable to his personalized strategic authority.","locator":"Paragraphs corresponding to web lines 70-75, especially the statement at line 73","period_fit":"Contemporaneous analysis published four months after the invasion.","attribution_limits":"Expert analysis based partly on opaque elite dynamics, not a disclosed decision memorandum or adjudicated finding.","contrary_evidence":"The analysis also notes elite adaptation and ideological sympathy with Putin's reasoning; it does not establish that all elites opposed the decision."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-003","canonical_fact_key":"rus_2022_aggression_against_ukraine_un_finding","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","3B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3965290","publisher":"United Nations General Assembly","publisher_date":"2022-03-02","claim":"The UN General Assembly adopted A/RES/ES-11/1 by 141 votes to 5, characterizing Russia's action as aggression and demanding an immediate end to military operations and unconditional withdrawal.","locator":"Document A/RES/ES-11/1; vote summary and operative paragraphs 1-4","period_fit":"Direct multilateral record eleven days after the full-scale invasion began.","attribution_limits":"A General Assembly resolution is an authoritative collective political and legal assessment but is not a criminal judgment against Putin personally.","contrary_evidence":"Russia and four other states voted against the resolution and Russia publicly advanced collective-self-defence and security justifications; the overwhelming majority rejected those positions."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-004","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_partial_mobilization_decree","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","4B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","3B.*","4B.*","6B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://publication.pravo.gov.ru/Document/View/0001202209210001","publisher":"Official Internet Portal of Legal Information, Russian Federation","publisher_date":"2022-09-21","claim":"Putin issued Presidential Decree No. 647 declaring partial mobilization in the Russian Federation from 21 September 2022.","locator":"Decree No. 647, title and operative clauses 1-10","period_fit":"Primary legal act issued and effective during the target year.","attribution_limits":"Proves Putin's formal decision and the mobilization framework, not the eventual number mobilized, implementation quality, fairness, casualties, or public support.","contrary_evidence":"The decree described mobilization as partial and assigned implementation to the government, Defence Ministry, and regional executives; subsequent claims about scope require independent verification."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-005","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_attempted_annexation_ukraine_regions","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","4B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","3B.*","4B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/statements/2022-09-29/secretary-generals-remarks-russian-decision-annexation-of-ukrainian-territory-delivered","publisher":"United Nations Secretary-General","publisher_date":"2022-09-29","claim":"The UN Secretary-General stated that Russia's planned annexation of four occupied Ukrainian regions would have no legal value, violated the UN Charter, and followed purported referendums conducted during armed conflict and occupation rather than a genuine expression of popular will.","locator":"Paragraphs beginning 'Any annexation of a State’s territory' through 'They cannot be called a genuine expression of the popular will'","period_fit":"Issued one day before Putin's 30 September accession ceremony.","attribution_limits":"Establishes the UN position on legality and conditions; it does not independently attribute every occupation practice to Putin personally.","contrary_evidence":"Russian authorities asserted that the votes expressed self-determination and that accession was lawful; the UN expressly rejected that characterization."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-006","canonical_fact_key":"rus_authorities_2022_ukraine_war_crimes_coi","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.*","2B.*","3B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/documents/hrbodies/hrcouncil/coiukraine/coI-ukraine-graphic_report-en.pdf","publisher":"UN Independent International Commission of Inquiry on Ukraine","publisher_date":"2023-03","claim":"After investigating conduct during 2022, the Commission found that Russian authorities committed war crimes including killings, attacks on civilians, unlawful confinement, torture, sexual violence, and unlawful transfers or deportations of children.","locator":"Page headed 'Main Findings' and accompanying 'Fact-Finding Methodology'","period_fit":"Retrospective inquiry focused substantially on conduct during the 2022 target period.","attribution_limits":"Findings concern Russian authorities and armed forces; this source alone does not establish Putin's knowledge, orders, intent, or individual criminal responsibility.","contrary_evidence":"The Commission also documented a smaller number of violations by Ukrainian forces, including likely indiscriminate attacks and mistreatment of prisoners."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-007","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_signed_wartime_expression_laws","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.*","4B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/07/russia-criminalizes-independent-war-reporting-anti-war-protests","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publisher_date":"2022-03-07; updated 2022-03-23","claim":"Putin signed and brought into force on 4 March two rapidly adopted laws criminalizing independent war reporting and anti-war expression, with maximum imprisonment of 15 years.","locator":"Web lines 605-613 and 626-629; especially lines 609 and 612","period_fit":"Contemporaneous reporting on legislation enacted eight days after the invasion.","attribution_limits":"Provides a direct personal nexus through Putin's signature, but legislative adoption also involved both parliamentary chambers and enforcement involved multiple institutions.","contrary_evidence":"Russian authorities framed the provisions as targeting knowingly false information and discreditation rather than truthful reporting; HRW concluded their breadth and censorship effects were incompatible with expression rights."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-008","canonical_fact_key":"rus_2022_restricted_information_environment","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.*","4B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://rsf.org/en/analyse_regionale/694","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publisher_date":"2022 country assessment","claim":"RSF reported that independent television was barred, major remaining independent outlets suspended publication, surviving media faced strict self-censorship, and nearly two-thirds of Russians relied mainly on government-controlled television or domestic social media for news.","locator":"Sections 'Media landscape,' 'Political context,' 'Legal framework,' and 'Sociocultural context'; web lines 313-327","period_fit":"Country assessment specifically describing Russia's 2022 media environment.","attribution_limits":"Country-level media conditions cannot automatically be treated as a personal act by Putin; use principally to qualify polling, approval, and public-information evidence.","contrary_evidence":"Internet penetration remained high, exiled and online independent media continued operating, and restricted access did not eliminate all alternative information."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-009","canonical_fact_key":"rus_2022_financial_shock_and_policy_stabilization","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["5B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.*","6B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://www.cbr.ru/Collection/Collection/File/46299/ar_2022_e.pdf","publisher":"Bank of Russia","publisher_date":"2023 annual report covering 2022","claim":"The Bank of Russia described 2022 as an exceptionally difficult year in which sanctions severely affected the economy and financial sector, while central-bank measures maintained financial-system stability.","locator":"Annual Report for 2022, introductory overview and section 'Ensuring Financial Stability'","period_fit":"Primary institutional retrospective covering the complete target year.","attribution_limits":"Useful for the shock and institutional response, but the central bank is an interested state institution; its operational success should not be credited solely to Putin or treated as proof of household welfare.","contrary_evidence":"The report documents stabilization rather than the economic collapse initially forecast, but also acknowledges sanctions, disrupted markets, and contraction in economic activity."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-010","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_elite_constraints_and_political_paralysis","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/russia-eurasia/politika/2022/06/has-war-made-putin-a-burden-for-russias-elites","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace / Tatiana Stanovaya","publisher_date":"2022-06-16","claim":"The analysis found Putin's wartime authority increasingly fragile, but also judged elite collective action against him extremely unlikely because political mechanisms had been destroyed, elites were atomized, and Putin remained their only available arbiter.","locator":"Web lines 75-83 and 87-93","period_fit":"Contemporaneous assessment of practical authority and constraints during 2022.","attribution_limits":"Analytical interpretation of elite behavior, not direct survey evidence; claims about private elite attitudes carry uncertainty.","contrary_evidence":"The same analysis predicted growing elite autonomy and declining reliance on Putin, indicating implementation constraints and weakening arbitration even without an organized challenge."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RUS-PUTIN-2022-RECON-011","canonical_fact_key":"rus_putin_2022_repressive_apparatus_and_personalized_security_control","disposition":"accepted","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.*","4B.*","7B.*","8B.*"],"url":"https://www.rusi.org/explore-our-research/publications/commentary/can-russia-police-its-protests-and-its-elites","publisher":"Royal United Services Institute","publisher_date":"2022-11-30","claim":"RUSI assessed that Putin retained a capable repressive apparatus and had personalized and fragmented security-sector control in ways that impeded both protest escalation and elite collective action against him.","locator":"Web lines 38-45 and 52-64","period_fit":"Contemporaneous analysis following the September mobilization protests.","attribution_limits":"Institutional analysis, not proof that Putin personally ordered each arrest or policing action; future stability projections were explicitly uncertain.","contrary_evidence":"RUSI noted wartime strain, refusals within the National Guard, declining legitimacy risks, and the possibility that broader opposition could make repression harder."}

Sources opened and retained include the Russian presidency, official legal-publication portal, UN General Assembly and Secretary-General, OHCHR Commission of Inquiry, Human Rights Watch, Reporters Without Borders, Bank of Russia, Carnegie, and RUSI.

Chapter gaps and next searches

- 1B: Military decision chain, pre-invasion intelligence, operational objectives, losses, nuclear signaling, and Putin’s response to battlefield reversals. Prioritize official orders, documented command structures, RUSI/IISS, and later judicial or commission findings.
- 2B: Separate initiated aggression, conduct of hostilities, diplomacy, ceasefire opportunities, annexation, and escalation control. Use UN, OSCE, ICJ, OHCHR, and credible military histories.
- 3B: Domestic repression versus abuses in occupied Ukraine; documented enforcement patterns, protest detentions, mobilization coercion, filtration, torture, and deportation. Preserve attribution distinctions.
- 4B: Legislative compliance, judicial independence, opposition exclusion, federal-regional authority, mobilization protests, and genuine public participation under censorship.
- 5B: Real GDP, sectoral production, inflation, investment, trade diversion, capital controls, energy receipts, and the counterfactual role of sanctions and commodity prices.
- 6B: Real household income, poverty, regional and ethnic mobilization burdens, mortality, health access, education disruption, emigration, and inequality.
- 7B: No adequate personal-integrity finding yet. Search only for documented Putin-specific enrichment, conflicts of interest, misuse of office, concealment, or adjudicated/official investigative findings; do not convert regime corruption into personal guilt.
- 8B: Decision quality, warning uptake, coordination, mobilization execution, institutional delegation, economic adaptation, stated objectives versus results, and whether failures are personally attributable or implementation constraints.
